[00:00:04.089] Browser LOG     [Interviewer] Question data: {"aiCustomPrompt":null,"aiHelperName":null,"aiPromptTemplate":"helpful","category":"algorithm","createdAt":"2025-12-19T10:13:16.75455","currentStep":null,"deactivated":null,"deactivatedAt":null,"description":"Build a rate limiter that controls how many requests are allowed in a time window.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nconst limiter = new RateLimiter(3, 1000); // 3 requests per second\nlimiter.allowRequest(); // true\nlimiter.allowRequest(); // true\nlimiter.allowRequest(); // true\nlimiter.allowRequest(); // false (limit reached)\n// After 1 second passes, requests are allowed again","difficulty":"medium","followUpQuestions":[{"answer":"The timestamps array would grow to 10,000 entries, using a lot of memory. We should consider a more memory-efficient approach.","id":1113,"orderIndex":0,"question":"What happens if this API gets 10,000 requests per second?"},{"answer":"You could divide time into buckets and store counts instead of timestamps. Sliding Window Counter - divide window into smaller buckets, store count per bucket, sum recent buckets. This achieves O(k) space where k = number of buckets, independent of traffic.","id":1114,"orderIndex":1,"question":"Can you think of a solution that uses fixed memory regardless of traffic?"},{"answer":"Current solution is precise but uses O(n) memory. Fixed-memory solutions are approximate but use O(1) memory. Current solution trades precision and simplicity for memory usage. Fixed-memory solutions like sliding window counter offer better scalability but sacrifice precision at bucket boundaries.","id":1115,"orderIndex":2,"question":"What's the tradeoff between your current solution and a fixed-memory solution?"},{"answer":"You would need to store the rate limiter state in a shared location like Redis. Use Redis or a distributed cache to store the rate limiter state. Handle race conditions with atomic operations or Lua scripts. Consider eventual consistency trade-offs.","id":1116,"